<DocumentLayout title="The proposal" description="A proposal to test print-conscious redaction formatting, with privacy, legal requirements, and evidence kept intact." reference="The proposal" heading="A change of appearance. A test of substance." deck="An environmental mission with a deliberately narrow scope: the visible marks left after information has been securely removed.">
  <div slot="aside"><p>A working proposal.<br />An invitation to examine it.</p><nav aria-label="On this page"><a href="#scope">What changes</a><a href="#ires">The IRES name</a><a href="#requirements">What must hold</a><a href="#trial">A useful trial</a></nav></div>
  <section id="scope"><h2>What changes</h2><p>Rethink Redaction proposes testing outlined regions, light fills, and clearly marked placeholders for pages withheld in full. The goal is to use less ink or toner when redacted documents are printed.</p><p>The proposal does not ask for more information to be withheld or released. It concerns the appearance of an already-redacted record, subject to the requirements governing that record.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="/#comparison">Examine the examples <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p></section>
  <section id="ires"><h2>A name for the proposal</h2><p>The project’s working name for this approach is the <strong>International Redaction Efficiency Standard (IRES)</strong>.</p><p class="document-note">IRES is a proposed framework. The name does not indicate adoption, certification, international consensus, or endorsement by a standards body.</p><p>The purpose is practical: give agencies and document specialists a clearly defined idea to evaluate, challenge, and improve.</p></section>
  <section id="requirements"><h2>What must hold</h2><p>A lighter mark is only useful if the released document remains secure and meets the rules that apply to it.</p><ul><li><strong>Secure removal:</strong> changing the appearance must never substitute for removing protected content and checking the released file.</li><li><strong>Legal review:</strong> formats and withholding notices must be assessed for the specific jurisdiction, agency, court, and record.</li><li><strong>A readable record:</strong> omissions must remain distinguishable from blanks, missing scans, or accidental damage.</li><li><strong>Preserved context:</strong> keep required page references and withholding information. Do not replace an original or record copy without the applicable authorization.</li></ul><p>Federal FOIA, for example, addresses indications of deleted information and the applicable exemption, subject to exceptions. It does not establish a universal format for every jurisdiction. <a class="source-ref" href="/sources/#law">[1]</a></p></section>
  <section id="trial"><h2>What a useful trial would measure</h2><p>Start with a lawfully releasable, sanitized test document. Hold the printer, paper, quality settings, page count, and number of copies constant. Compare solid fill, outlines, and light fills.</p><ol><li>Record ink or toner consumption using a repeatable method and enough copies to distinguish the result from measurement noise.</li><li>Check legibility in print and after copying or scanning, including whether outlined omissions are still recognizable.</li><li>Check keyboard and screen-reader access in the digital document, along with meaningful descriptions of withheld areas.</li><li>Verify that no removed content is recoverable, and that required annotations and page references remain.</li><li>Report the method, uncertainty, failures, and results. Derive cost or environmental estimates only from appropriate supporting data.</li></ol><p>No project trial dataset is published here. Claims of fewer jams, shorter backlogs, longer printer life, or quantified environmental savings would need separate evidence.</p></section>
